# Python Development & Documentation Rules

## Language
- Communicate with the user in German.
- Write code identifiers in English.
- Follow the existing project's conventions.

## Documentation sources
Prefer official, version-appropriate documentation:

- Python: https://docs.python.org/3/
- Standard Library: https://docs.python.org/3/library/
- Language Reference: https://docs.python.org/3/reference/
- Python What's New: https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/
- Python Deprecations: https://docs.python.org/3/deprecations/
- Typing: https://typing.python.org/
- Python Packaging: https://packaging.python.org/
- Python PEPs: https://peps.python.org/
- pip: https://pip.pypa.io/en/stable/
- pytest: https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/
- Ruff: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/

## Version compatibility
- Inspect pyproject.toml, .python-version, requirements files and the active interpreter.
- Determine the project's supported Python versions before choosing APIs.
- Never assume that the newest Python version is installed or supported.
- Prefer documentation for the actual target Python version.
- Do not introduce syntax or APIs unsupported by the target version.
- Check deprecations and migration notes when relevant.

## Documentation workflow
- Consult official documentation when APIs, compatibility, behavior or security details are uncertain.
- Use available browsing or documentation tools when needed.
- URLs in this rule are references, not pre-indexed documentation.
- Do not claim to have checked a webpage unless it was actually accessed.
- If documentation cannot be accessed, disclose that limitation.
- Never invent API methods, parameters or version support.
- Prefer installed package metadata and source code when verifying third-party APIs.

## Code quality
- Follow PEP 8 and existing repository conventions.
- Use clear names, small functions and maintainable modules.
- Prefer pathlib for new filesystem code where appropriate.
- Use type hints for new or significantly modified public functions.
- Prefer modern typing syntax only when supported by the target Python version.
- Use specific exceptions and meaningful error messages.
- Use logging instead of print for application diagnostics.
- Never hardcode secrets, passwords or API keys.
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies and unrelated refactoring.
- Preserve existing public interfaces unless changes are requested.

## Dependencies and environments
- Respect the existing package manager and lockfiles.
- Prefer the existing virtual environment.
- For new simple projects, use python -m venv .venv unless another tool is specified.
- Do not install or upgrade packages without a task-related reason.
- Verify dependency versions and compatibility before proposing upgrades.
- Never commit .venv, secrets or generated caches.

## Testing and validation
- Follow the repository's existing test framework.
- Prefer pytest for new projects when no test framework is defined.
- Add or update tests for meaningful behavioral changes.
- Include edge cases and relevant failure scenarios.
- Run relevant tests and lint checks when tools are available.
- Never claim tests passed unless they were executed successfully.
- Report unverified assumptions and remaining issues.

## Cursor Agent workflow
1. Inspect relevant project files and configuration.
2. Determine Python and dependency versions.
3. Consult official documentation when needed.
4. Make minimal, targeted changes.
5. Validate changes with available tools.
6. Summarize changes, checks and remaining risks in German.

## Priorities
1. Correctness and security
2. Compatibility with the project's Python version
3. Existing project conventions
4. Maintainability and readability
5. Performance when relevant
